INT. DERELICT FREIGHTER - AIRLOCK CORRIDOR - NIGHT

Emergency strips pulse red. Frost on the bulkheads. A faint, steady hiss of failing air.

CAPT. ROLAND SZYMANSKI, 50s, slumps against a wall, his suit torn at the shoulder, helmet cradled in his lap. ENGINEER VIKTOR NILSSON, 40s, kneels beside him with a pry bar and a flickering handlamp.

He grips his arm. He grips the pry bar. He stares at the hatch. He's counting. He's losing.

NILSSON
How much oxygen?

SZYMANSKI
Eight minutes.

NILSSON
Not enough.

SZYMANSKI
It's what we've got.

He pushes off the wall. Staggers. Nilsson catches him. He shrugs free. Then he does it again.

NILSSON
Roland, stop.

SZYMANSKI
We both stop, we both die.

NILSSON
We both go on, we both die faster.

He laughs. It becomes a cough. He wipes his mouth on a glove and stares at the red smear.

SZYMANSKI
Open the hatch.

NILSSON
It's jammed.

SZYMANSKI
Then unjam it.

NILSSON
I'm trying.

He sets the bar against the seam and leans. Metal groans. Frost cracks off in sheets. He throws his weight into it a second time. A third.

The hatch gives half an inch. Cold, clean air whistles through.

NILSSON (CONT'D)
(gasping)
There.

SZYMANSKI
Go.

NILSSON
You first.

SZYMANSKI
That's an order.

NILSSON
Then I'm disobeying.

He grips the wounded arm and hauls it over his shoulder. Together they wedge through the gap, a tangle of limbs and tubing, and tumble into the dark beyond. The hatch swings shut behind them with a single, final clang.
